---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const faqSchema = {
 "@context": "https://schema.org",
 "@type": "FAQPage",
 "mainEntity": [
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Does NAT protect me from hackers?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "It blocks unsolicited inbound connections, which is real protection, but it is not a firewall you configure: outbound threats and phishing pass freely."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Why does IPv6 remove NAT?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Every device gets a globally routable address, so translation is unnecessary; the same protection comes from proper firewalls."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is CGNAT?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Carrier-grade NAT: your ISP applies the same trick to whole neighborhoods, stacking another layer. It breaks port forwarding entirely and puts hundreds of users behind one address."
   }
  }
 ]
}
---
<BaseLayout
  title="NAT Explained: How One Public IP Serves a Whole Network | PromptDNS"
  description="NAT (Network Address Translation) explained: how routers share one public IP among many devices, what NAT breaks, and its role in IPv4 survival."
  schema={[faqSchema]}
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/ip-and-networking/" class="hover:text-accent">IP & Networking</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">NAT Explained</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">NAT Explained</h1>
    <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Network Address Translation is the trick that let the internet survive IPv4 exhaustion: your router rewrites addresses so an entire household shares one public IP, and somehow everything still works.</p>
    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">How it works</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5"><li class="leading-relaxed">A device inside sends a packet out; the router replaces its private source address (192.168.1.x) with the public IP and records the connection in a translation table.</li><li class="leading-relaxed">Replies come back to the public IP; the router matches them to the table entry and forwards to the original device.</li><li class="leading-relaxed">Unsolicited inbound packets with no table entry are dropped: that is why NAT is accidental firewall protection, and why hosting behind it needs port forwarding.</li></ul>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">What NAT breaks and why</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5"><li class="leading-relaxed">Inbound hosting: nothing can reach your devices until you forward ports.</li><li class="leading-relaxed">End-to-end applications: VoIP, P2P, and some games need NAT traversal (STUN/TURN).</li><li class="leading-relaxed">Mail sending: consumer NAT space is blocklisted by policy (that is what Spamhaus PBL tracks), so mail must relay through a smarthost.</li></ul>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3"><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Does NAT protect me from hackers?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">It blocks unsolicited inbound connections, which is real protection, but it is not a firewall you configure: outbound threats and phishing pass freely.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Why does IPv6 remove NAT?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Every device gets a globally routable address, so translation is unnecessary; the same protection comes from proper firewalls.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">What is CGNAT?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Carrier-grade NAT: your ISP applies the same trick to whole neighborhoods, stacking another layer. It breaks port forwarding entirely and puts hundreds of users behind one address.</p></details></div>
      
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3"><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">What is nat explained in simple terms?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Think of it as the rulebook that a specific part of the internet follows. Every page in this section explains one rule, why it exists, and how to follow it correctly.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Do I need to memorize all of this?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">No: bookmark this page and the related tools. The goal is understanding what to check and when, not memorizing syntax.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">How long does it take to implement?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Most configurations take 5-15 minutes in your DNS panel. The verification (using our tools) takes another 2 minutes.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">What happens if I get it wrong?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Depends on the setting: some errors cause immediate visible failures, others silently degrade deliverability or security. Every guide includes the symptoms to watch for.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Is this the same across all DNS providers?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">The underlying protocol is identical; the dashboard layout and terminology differ. Our provider playbooks cover the exact flow for Cloudflare, GoDaddy, Namecheap, Route 53, and others.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Where can I get help if I'm stuck?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Each guide links to the relevant free tool so you can verify your work. The support page lists email channels for questions the guides don't cover.</p></details></div>
<h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Related</h2>
      <ul class="mt-3 grid gap-2"><li><a href="/my-ip/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">What Is My IP →</a></li><li><a href="/ipv4-vs-ipv6/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">IPv4 vs IPv6 →</a></li><li><a href="/public-vs-private-ip/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">Public vs private IP →</a></li></ul>

    <div class="mt-10 flex items-center gap-3 rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/40 px-5 py-3">
      <span class="grid h-9 w-9 place-items-center rounded-full bg-gradient-to-br from-accent to-violet font-display text-xs font-bold text-canvas">PD</span>
      <div>
        <p class="text-sm font-medium text-ink">PromptDNS Team</p>
        <p class="text-xs text-ink-faint">Last updated: August 2026</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
